  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Pictorial Ledger",
    "overview": "Moni counts money the way Otto Neurath and Gerd Arntz counted populations: every quantity is a row of identical unit figures, one figure equals one fixed amount, and more money means more figures, never a bigger figure. \"How am I doing\" and \"where does it go\" are read by row length before a single number is parsed. The page is paper and ink, with four flat domain inks that mean the same thing on every screen.\n\nDensity is calm. Sections are separated by an ink hairline and generous vertical air (56px), not by cards. Hierarchy comes from scale alone: one huge patrimonio numeral, then section titles, then body. There are no gradients, no glow, no tinted surfaces and no decorative imagery; the pictograms are the only pictures, and each one is also a unit of measure.\n\nThe world ships a dark rendition (ink-black ground, the same four inks lifted for contrast) that follows the system preference. It is the same world, not a second theme.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Isotype rows of identical flat silhouettes as the primary data display.",
      "Four fixed domain inks: blue cartera, green inversiones, ochre activos, red deudas.",
      "One typeface (Jost, Futura lineage) for every word and number, tabular figures throughout.",
      "Ink hairlines above sections; a thick ink underline marks the active tab or period.",
      "Flat everywhere: no card surfaces, no shadows, no gradients."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Fixed Ink Rule",
        "body": "Blue is always cartera, green always inversiones, ochre always activos, red always deudas or subtraction. A domain ink is never used decoratively or for a different meaning, and no fifth data color is introduced.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Red Subtracts Rule",
        "body": "Anything that lowers net worth or cash is red, and the legend says so (\"Lo rojo resta\"). Spending is ink, not red; red is reserved for debt and loss.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ochre Split Rule",
        "body": "Ochre fills figures; Activos Text carries ochre words and amounts. Never set small text in the fill ochre on paper.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Scale-Only Hierarchy Rule",
        "body": "Rank is expressed by size and weight, never by a colored or boxed heading. There is exactly one display-size number per screen.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Rule",
        "body": "Numbers are explained by a full sentence beneath them (\"Te quedó el 63,4% de lo que entró.\"), not by a label above them.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No-Card Rule",
        "body": "There are no card containers. Grouping is done with a full-width ink rule above a section and whitespace around it.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Focus-Dims Rule",
        "body": "Emphasis comes from dimming the rest, not lifting the subject: an open spending category keeps full ink while every other row drops to 25% opacity over 300ms.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do show any comparable set of quantities as figure rows on one shared unit, with a key line stating the unit.",
      "Do keep each domain on its fixed ink and pictogram (activos ochre house, inversiones green plant, cartera blue banknote, deudas red kettlebell) on every tab.",
      "Do separate sections with a 1px ink rule and 56px of space, and dividers inside lists with the rule color.",
      "Do mark selection with an ink bar (3px for navigation, 2px for toggles) and hover with a wash fill.",
      "Do explain a figure with a full sentence in 15px body or 13px label, emphasizing the number in ink.",
      "Do set every amount in Jost with tabular figures, signed with a true minus sign.",
      "Do carry every new color decision as a light and dark pair."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't scale a figure up to show more money; add figures.",
      "Don't wrap content in cards, tinted panels or bordered boxes.",
      "Don't use gradients, glow or decorative drop shadows.",
      "Don't introduce a second typeface or a monospace for amounts.",
      "Don't use a domain ink for anything outside its domain, or set small text in the fill ochre on paper.",
      "Don't fall back to the KPI-tile row, donut chart or smoothed line chart."
    ]
  }
}